course: dictionary
lesson: 10180
title: Tightly: firm, close or strict
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tightly is an adverb, a word that tells you how something is done. It has three main meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Read the words next to it first.
explain: Firmly and closely: «Hold the rope tightly.» Here tightly means so it does not move or come loose. Try the swap: "Hold the rope firmly" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: With no space: «The penguins' feathers were tightly packed.» Tightly means very close together, with no gaps. The word packed is a clue that things are squashed together.
explain: Strictly: «The plan was tightly controlled.» Here tightly means watched or limited very strictly. This is not about holding or squeezing at all. Ask yourself: is something being held, or is something being kept under strict rules? That question tells you which meaning you need.
fact: Tightly means firmly and closely, so it does not move or come loose.
fact: «Tightly packed» means packed with no space between things.
fact: «Tightly controlled» means watched or limited very strictly.
fact: «Penguins also keep warm with a thick layer of fat and tightly packed waterproof feathers.»
fact: «Çatalhöyük in Turkey was a town about 9,000 years ago with houses so tightly packed that people walked over the roofs and climbed in through holes with ladders.»
exercise: «Hold the rope tightly.» What does tightly mean here? | Loosely and slowly | *Firmly, so it does not come loose | Quietly | Far away
exercise: «A crowd squeezing too tightly can crush people.» What does tightly suggest? | Lifting up into the air | *Pressing with no space between people | Singing loudly | Moving slowly away from the road
exercise: «Hold a younger child's hand tightly near roads.» Why? | So they can wave to friends | So their hand gets cold | *So they stay safe and do not run into the road | So they can play a game
test: «Shut the bottle tightly after use.» What does tightly mean here? | Brightly | Slowly | *Firmly, so it cannot come open | Loosely
test: Which phrase means with no space between things? | Loudly sung | Slowly walked | Softly lit | *Tightly packed
exam: Lena says a «tightly controlled» plan means a loose and free plan. Is she right? | No, tightly controlled means watched or limited very strictly | No, tightly controlled means very loud | *Yes, tightly controlled means very relaxed and free | Yes, tightly controlled means very old
